  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nLow to medium rain chances will continue over the Coastal Plains \ntoday as another shortwave moves across the area. By Thursday, the \nremnants of Pacific Tropical Storm Vance will move into South Texas, \nwith the highest moisture along the Rio Grande. This will shift \nmedium rain chances over the Brush Country and Rio Grande Plains. \nWPC has placed the Brush Country under a Marginal Risk (level 1 out \nof 4) of excessive rainfall Thursday, with a Slight Risk (level 2 \nout of 4) extending into northwestern portions of the region. \nLocally heavy rainfall and isolated flash flooding will be possible, \nespecially where storms become persistent or training develops. \nIsolated flooding and ponding will be possible in low lying poor \ndrainage areas. However, uncertainty remains regarding the location \nand magnitude of the heaviest rainfall. Rain chances will diminish \nto low Friday into the weekend as the remnants move to the \nnortheast. Fortunately, rain chances look like they will be out of \nthe area by the evening Saturday night and we will remain dry \nthrough the rest of the weekend.\n\nAnother cold front is possible Tuesday, which could increase our \nrain chances once again. For now, we will continue to focus on the \nbeneficial rain this week.\n\nMinor coastal flooding remains a concern through much of the week as \npersistent onshore flow, elevated swell periods, and astronomical \ntides contribute to higher water levels during high tide. An \nincreased risk of rip currents will also persist.",
